This bytecode VM has two bugs that only bite on nested control flow. Write failing pytest tests reproducing both, then fix vm.py and keep the tests green.

OPS = {'PUSH':1,'ADD':0,'SUB':0,'LT':0,'JMP':1,'JZ':1,'PRINT':0,'HALT':0}

def run(code):
    out, stack, ip = [], [], 0
    while True:
        op = code[ip]
        if op == 'PUSH':
            stack.append(code[ip+1]); ip += 2
        elif op == 'ADD':
            a = stack.pop(); b = stack.pop(); stack.append(a + b); ip += 1
        elif op == 'SUB':
            a = stack.pop(); b = stack.pop(); stack.append(a - b); ip += 1
        elif op == 'LT':
            a = stack.pop(); b = stack.pop(); stack.append(1 if a < b else 0); ip += 1
        elif op == 'JMP':
            ip = code[ip+1]
        elif op == 'JZ':
            ip = code[ip+1] if stack.pop() == 0 else ip + 1
        elif op == 'PRINT':
            out.append(stack.pop()); ip += 1
        elif op == 'HALT':
            return out

Bug 1: SUB and LT pop operands in the wrong order, so PUSH 10, PUSH 3, SUB yields -7 instead of 7 and comparisons invert. Bug 2: JZ, when the condition is nonzero, advances ip by 1 instead of skipping its operand, so execution lands on the jump target as if it were an opcode. Demonstrate each with a minimal program (include a countdown loop 3 2 1 using LT/JZ/JMP that currently misbehaves), then fix and rerun until all pass.

